WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:01.253
This is aos:

00:00:01.253 --> 00:00:04.905
one small tool that gives your AI agent hands.

00:00:04.905 --> 00:00:10.939
Agents can write code all day, but they can't move a window, click a button,

00:00:10.939 --> 00:00:13.004
or show you where to look.

00:00:13.004 --> 00:00:18.800
aos puts the whole desktop behind one command, on Mac, Windows and Linux.

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:21.995
Getting it is a single line.

00:00:21.995 --> 00:00:27.410
The installer fetches the right release for your machine, verifies the checksum,

00:00:27.410 --> 00:00:29.440
and drops one binary in place.

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:31.200
No toolchain, and no root.

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:35.062
The first thing to run is aos doctor.

00:00:35.062 --> 00:00:39.148
It checks that this machine can actually keep its promises:

00:00:39.148 --> 00:00:44.550
screen access, permissions, the clipboard, even which package manager is here.

00:00:45.867 --> 00:00:48.659
Let's open a note and bring in Calculator.

00:00:48.659 --> 00:00:53.179
Instead of dragging windows around, just tell aos where they belong:

00:00:53.179 --> 00:00:57.567
TextEdit in the top right quarter, Calculator in the bottom right.

00:00:58.867 --> 00:01:00.493
Got a layout you like?

00:01:00.493 --> 00:01:02.267
Keep it in a small file.

00:01:02.267 --> 00:01:04.559
Then, however messy things get,

00:01:04.559 --> 00:01:09.217
one arrange call puts every window right back where it belongs.

00:01:10.533 --> 00:01:13.228
Now, where exactly is the seven key?

00:01:13.228 --> 00:01:19.366
aos find asks the accessibility tree, falls back to on-screen text when it has to,

00:01:19.366 --> 00:01:23.183
and hands back the precise spot, ready for a click.

00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:30.445
And when a person needs to see it, aos draw puts a big arrow right on the screen.

00:01:30.445 --> 00:01:31.325
Press seven.

00:01:31.325 --> 00:01:37.050
It's click-through, so it never gets in your way, and it fades out on its own.

00:01:38.367 --> 00:01:40.871
The keyboard works the same way.

00:01:40.871 --> 00:01:47.134
aos key type writes straight into TextEdit, and key press sends any combination,

00:01:47.134 --> 00:01:49.717
from enter, to command S to save.

00:01:51.033 --> 00:01:53.445
Screenshots are one command too:

00:01:53.445 --> 00:01:56.385
a window, a region, or a whole monitor.

00:01:56.385 --> 00:02:00.983
Here's that TextEdit window, captured, and opened in Preview.

00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:03.679
Away from your desk?

00:02:03.679 --> 00:02:09.816
aos remote share streams this screen to any browser on your network, your phone included.

00:02:09.816 --> 00:02:14.850
The link carries a one time key, and it dies the moment you stop sharing.

00:02:16.167 --> 00:02:17.760
But here's the real point:

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:19.967
you don't have to learn any of this.

00:02:19.967 --> 00:02:22.296
Just ask Claude Code in plain English.

00:02:22.296 --> 00:02:25.852
Here I'm asking it to point at the nine key on Calculator.

00:02:25.852 --> 00:02:30.817
Watch it find the button, pick the right aos commands, and draw the arrow for me.

00:02:32.133 --> 00:02:34.544
It works without a chat window, too.

00:02:34.544 --> 00:02:38.294
Run claude with dash p from a script or a scheduled job,

00:02:38.294 --> 00:02:41.576
and the same agent drives the desktop on its own.

00:02:41.576 --> 00:02:46.933
Here it takes a screenshot of our note, reads it, and reports back what it says.

00:02:48.233 --> 00:02:49.137
That's aos.

00:02:49.137 --> 00:02:54.394
One binary, every desktop, and an agent that can finally use it.

00:02:54.394 --> 00:03:00.883
Grab it at muthuishere dot github dot io, slash aos, and give your agent hands.
